al":4.14,"confidence":{"publicFame":0.86,"criticalRenown":0.78,"controversy":0.8,"llmAffinity":0.88},"tags":{"craft":"craft-of-explanation","evidence":"synthesis-of-scholarship","stance":"wonder"},"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":1},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[{"name":"Einstein","confidence":0.98}],"centralPlaces":[{"name":"Japan","confidence":0.78}],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"The detection of gravitational waves confirms Einstein’s theory of general relativity and opens a new era of astronomy capable of revealing the universe’s structure and origin.","confidence":0.96},"academicOrientation":{"score":35,"confidence":0.88},"profileConfidence":0.93,"lowerConfidenceCandidates":{"centralPlaces":[{"name":"Chile","confidence":0.72},{"name":"South Pole","confidence":0.7}]},"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"d30c679f6fdffaae359a2aae2881ce4e6a72bb42f993dc79c55bbd3d8699535c","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"Science","secondarySubjects":[],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book primarily discusses the scientific breakthrough of detecting gravitational waves, framed within the context of astrophysics and Einstein's theories, making Science the dominant subject.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"06a670e6e0f0bab7f13b8336b0481d7d3f95a38726619275ded8d7272ab924b4","summary":"It has already been called the scientific breakthrough of the century: the detection of gravitational waves. Einstein predicted these tiny ripples in the fabric of spacetime nearly a hundred years ago, but they were never perceived directly until now. Decades in the making, this momentous discovery has given scientists a new understanding of the cataclysmic events that shape the universe and a new confirmation of Einstein's theory of general relativity. Ripples in Spacetime is an engaging account of the international effort to complete Einstein's project, capture his elusive ripples, and launch an era of gravitational-wave astronomy that promises to explain, more vividly than ever before, our universe's structure and origin. The quest for gravitational waves involved years of risky research and many personal and professional struggles that threatened to derail one of the world's largest scientific endeavors. Govert Schilling takes readers to sites where these stories unfolded--including Japan's KAGRA detector, Chile's Atacama Cosmology Telescope, the South Pole's BICEP detectors, and the United States' LIGO labs. He explains the seeming impossibility of developing technologies sensitive enough to detect waves from two colliding black holes in the very distant universe, and describes the astounding precision of the LIGO detectors.","publicationYear":2017,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Methodology","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-ripples-in-spacetime-einstein-gravitational-waves-and-the-future-of-astronomy-govert-schilling"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Gravitational waves","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-ripples-in-spacetime-einstein-gravitational-waves-and-the-future-of-astronomy-govert-schilling"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Relativistic astrophysics","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-ripples-in-spacetime-einstein-gravitational-waves-and-the-future-of-astronomy-govert-schilling"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Einstein, albert, 1879-1955","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-ripples-in-spacetime-einstein-gravitational-waves-and-the-future-of-astronomy-govert-schilling"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","label":"Astrophysics","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-ripples-in-spacetime-einstein-gravitational-waves-and-the-future-of-astronomy-govert-schilling"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","lab